@(A.1 = 1-5) Brickwork rising out  @varh[t1824.v1]{from the pristine mountain } @vart[t1824.v2,t1824.v1]{@varn{on the edge of ms. C:} of the shining plain } -- @wSN{Kulaba}, city which reaches from heaven to earth; @wSN{Unug}, whose fame like the rainbow reaches up to the sky, a multicoloured sheen, as the new moon standing in the heavens.

#etcsl#altGrpo
#etcsl#alt t1824.v1 t1824.v2
#etcsl#altGrpc

@(A.6 = 6-13) Built in magnificence with all the great powers, lustrous mount founded on a favourable day, like moonlight coming up over the land, like bright sunlight radiating over the land, the rear cow and …… cow coming forth in abundance: all this is @wSN{Unug}, the glory of which reaches the highland and its radiance, genuine refined silver, covers @wSN{Aratta} like a garment, is spread over it like linen.

@(A.14 = 14-24) At that time the day was lord, the night was sovereign, and @wDN{Utu} was king. Now the name of the lord of @wSN{Aratta's} minister was minister @wPN{Ansiga-ria}. The name of the minister of @wRN{Enmerkar}, the lord of @wSN{Kulaba}, was @wPN{Namena-tuma}. He with the …… lord, he with the …… prince; he with the …… lord, he with the …… prince; he with the …… lord, he with the …… prince; he with the man born to be a god; he with a man manifest as a god, with the lord of @wSN{Unug}, the lord of @wSN{Kulaba} -- @wRN{En-suḫgir-ana}, the lord of @wSN{Aratta}, is to make a contest with him, saying first to the messenger concerning @wSN{Unug}:

@(A.25 = 25-39) @q[En-suhgir-ana,messenger]{“Let him submit to me, let him bear my yoke. If he submits to me, indeed submits to me, then as for him and me -- he may dwell with @wDN{Inana} within a walled enclosure (?), but I dwell with @wDN{Inana} in the @wTN{E-zagin} of @wSN{Aratta}; he may lie with her on the splendid bed, but I lie in sweet slumber with her on the adorned bed, he may see dreams with @wDN{Inana} at night, but I converse with @wDN{Inana} awake. He may feed the geese with barley, but I will definitely not feed the geese with barley. I will …… the geese's eggs in a basket and …… their goslings. The small ones into my pot, the large ones into my kettle, and the rulers of the land who submitted will consume, together with me, what remains from the geese.”} This is what he said to @wRN{Enmerkar}.

@(A.40 = 40-51) The messenger runs like a wild ram and flies like a falcon. He leaves in the morning and returns already at dusk, like small birds at dawn, he …… over the open country, like small birds at midnight, he hides himself in the interior of the mountains. Like a throw-stick, he stands at the side. Like a solitary donkey of @wDN{Šakkan}, he  @varh[t1824.v3]{runs over } @vart[t1824.v4,t1824.v3]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} cuts through } the mountains, he dashes like a large, powerful donkey. A slim donkey, eager to run, he rushes forth. A lion in the field at dawn, he lets out roars; like a wolf which has seized a lamb, he runs quickly. The small places he has reached, he fills with …… for him; the large places he has reached, he …… boundary (?).

#etcsl#altGrpo
#etcsl#alt t1824.v3 t1824.v4
#etcsl#altGrpc

@(A.52 = 52-69) He entered the presence of the lord in  @varh[t1824.v5]{his holy @fsux{ŋipar} } @vart[t1824.v6,t1824.v5]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} in his most holy place }. @vart[t1824.v7,t1824.v7]{@varn{1 ms. adds 1 line:} He entered the presence of @wRN{Enmerkar} in his most holy place. } @q[messenger,Enmerkar]{“My king has sent me to you. The lord of @wSN{Aratta}, @wRN{En-suḫgir-ana}, has sent me to you.”}  @vart[t1824.v8,t1824.v8]{@varn{some mss. add the lines:} @q[Enmerkar,messenger]{“What does your king have to tell me, what does he have to add to me? What does @wRN{En-suḫgir-ana} have to tell me, what does he have to add to me?”} @q[messenger,Enmerkar]{“This is what my king said, what he added, this is what @wRN{En-suḫgir-ana} said, what he added.”} } @q[messenger,Enmerkar]{“This is what my king says: @q[En-suhgir-ana,messenger]{“Let him submit to me, let him bear my yoke. If he submits to me, indeed submits to me, then as for him and me -- he may dwell with @wDN{Inana} within a walled enclosure (?), but I dwell with @wDN{Inana} in the @wTN{E-zagin} of @wSN{Aratta}; he may lie with her on the splendid bed, but I lie in sweet slumber with her on the adorned bed, he may see dreams with @wDN{Inana} at night, but I converse with @wDN{Inana} awake. He may feed the geese with barley, but I will definitely not feed the geese with barley. I will …… the geese's eggs in a basket and …… their goslings. The small ones into my pot, the large ones into my kettle, and the rulers of the land who submitted will consume, together with me, what remains from the geese.”}”}

